C++20 iterator concepts evaluated over a user-defined *class* iterator: the deep
concept chain random_access_iterator -> bidirectional -> forward -> input ->
input_or_output_iterator, which descends through semiregular -> copyable ->
movable -> swappable -> assignable_from and totally_ordered /
sized_sentinel_for.  Depends on requirement-parameter binding for conjoined
requires-expressions ([expr.prim.req.general]/2).  Includes the negative case
(a non-incrementable type is not an iterator).  See
doc/architectural/cpp-requires-expression-support.md.

KNOWNBUG since round 152: main() was only partially type-checked and the
run reported SUCCESSFUL over the truncated body (user-reported Issue 13);
an incomplete user function is an error now.

2026-09-23: the test source's `p <=> o.p` had been reformatted to `p <= >
o.p` (rejected by g++ too; now guarded by clang-format off/on).
input_or_output_iterator<It> holds since the requires-parameter binding
fixes (a function template's parameters leaked into namespace std as a
secondary lookup scope; `__i` resolved ambiguously).  Remaining layers:
common_reference_with<int&, int&&> (see
cpp20_common_reference_with_cv_differing_references) and
__detail::__iter_concept<It> (the __iter_concept_impl partial
specializations), so input_iterator<It> still fails: "static assertion
failed: It input".
